{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_stock_metrics", "label": "Stock metrics (page)", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_metrics", "label": "Actor lifecycle and message metrics", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "actor_created_total, actor_terminated_total, actor_restarted_total, actor_messages_delivered_total and actor_message_handler_seconds, each a single unlabeled series."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_mailbox_size", "label": "actor_mailbox_size gauge", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Only actors at or above 10 000 queued messages get a series, so its presence is the alert and on a healthy system the metric is empty; the path label is constitutive rather than descriptive, because a depth gauge without it would have every actor of a class overwriting its siblings within one sampling pass."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_mailbox_depth", "label": "actor_mailbox_depth histogram", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The distribution the gauge cannot be: observed once per delivery so nothing is missed between samples, and label-free so the family costs one series per bucket however many actors exist. Its last bucket boundary is the gauge floor, so the +Inf overflow is by construction an actor the gauge already reports by path."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_mailbox_wait_seconds", "label": "actor_mailbox_wait_seconds histogram", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The latency half actor_message_handler_seconds cannot give you, since that starts measuring once a message is already being handled. Buckets run 1 ms to 10 s rather than the default ladder, because a mailbox keeping up drains in well under 5 ms. Replayed stashed messages and messages queued before metrics were enabled are excluded; throttled messages are counted."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_mailbox_dropped_total", "label": "actor_mailbox_dropped_total counter", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Counted per class, not per actor: a bounded mailbox sheds as its designed steady state, so a path-labelled counter minted a permanent series for every actor working as intended, and under sharding the value came from whoever addressed the shard region. Use your own onDrop, which fires alongside the stock counter, for per-instance detail."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_dispatcher_queue_delay_seconds", "label": "actor_dispatcher_queue_delay_seconds histogram", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The saturation signal, one observation per turn rather than per message. Buckets run 10 us to 10 s, two decades below the mailbox families, because a healthy hand-off is microseconds. MicrotaskDispatcher needs care: its queue is the microtask queue, so its delay stays low even while actors starve the event loop."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_why_there_is_no_saturation_ratio", "label": "Why there is no saturation ratio", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "performance.eventLoopUtilization is absent on Bun, real on Node and permanently zero on Deno, so a feature check passes on two runtimes and one of them lies; a ratio that reads a flat 0 % on Deno forever is worse than publishing nothing. Scheduling delay needs nothing but a clock, so it is the same measurement on all three."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_dead_letters_total", "label": "actor_dead_letters_total counter", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Minted by the queue, so it exists only once the store is not off; recipient is unbounded in principle and bounded in practice by the per-family cap, and it pays for itself because this counter only fires when something is already wrong. A loss signal, not a backlog one."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_cluster_metrics", "label": "Cluster metrics", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "cluster_members_up, cluster_gossip_rounds_total, cluster_gossip_records_refused_total{reason} and cluster_envelope_from_mismatch_total{frame}; the last two should sit at zero, and their label sets are closed."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_distributeddata_metrics", "label": "DistributedData metrics", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Five families in eight series over the CRDT quorum path, its wire decoder and its gossip packer; operation is write or read and reason is oversize or unserialisable, those values and no others."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_projection_metrics", "label": "Projection metrics", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A projection is a background loop nobody watches, which is exactly the kind of thing that fails quietly. persistence_projection_stalled is published as 0 at start so the series exists and == 1 is a working alert; separating reason=handler from reason=poll separates the two fixes, and events_skipped_total is the data-loss signal."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_cardinality_cap", "label": "The registry cardinality cap", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A family stops at 10 000 series and folds the rest into an __overflow__ label, so the process cannot be memory-exhausted through a label \u2014 but 10 000 series in one family is still a lot to scrape, and an __overflow__ series appearing is the signal that you are there."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_stock_label_rule", "label": "What may become a stock label", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A stock label value must be bounded by what the deployment declares \u2014 never by traffic volume, by how many actors have been spawned, or by a value a remote party supplies. class is a source-code constant, reason and frame are closed sets, projection is a name you write."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_noop_registry_overhead", "label": "No opt-out, a noop registry instead", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Stock metrics are always wired in; until enable() every instrumentation call resolves against a noop registry, a single object lookup that records nothing, so the cost is negligible and enabling swaps in the live registry."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_counters_reset_on_restart", "label": "Counters are per-process", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/metrics/stock-metrics.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Prometheus computes rates over the increasing series and handles resets gracefully, but raw counter values are not meaningful across restarts."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_stock_metrics", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_metrics_stock_metrics_actor_metrics", "relation": "references", "confidence": "E